 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Threshold lies between -1.7 V and -2.5 V (typically -1.9 V) at -250 µA. On-resistance is guaranteed at two gate voltages: 15 mΩ max at -10 V and 20 mΩ max at -4.5 V; at 125 °C junction and -10 V it is 23 mΩ max. The gate takes ±20 V at most and leaks no more than ±100 nA there. Charging the gate to -10 V takes 42 nC typical, to -4.5 V 18.6 nC; internal gate resistance is 2.5 to 6 Ω, and every part is screened for it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat and current",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "All thermal and ambient-current figures assume 1 in² of 2 oz copper on FR-4 in still air at 25 °C. There RθJA is 40 °C/W max for periods up to 10 s and 75 °C/W max held steady; junction to lead is 24 °C/W max. That gives -12 A and 3.1 W for 10 s, or -10 A and 1.7 W continuously, at 25 °C ambient, falling to -8 A and 1.1 W at 70 °C. On-resistance at 150 °C junction is about 1.6 times its 25 °C value (Figure 4)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Avalanche",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Every part is tested in unclamped inductive switching. The avalanche ratings are -28 A and 118 mJ with a 0.3 mH inductor, at a frequency and duty cycle low enough to hold Tj at 25 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulsed current and safe operating area",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Pulsed drain current is -120 A, a repetitive figure bounded by junction temperature. Figures 9 to 11 are taken on the same 1 in² copper board in still 25 °C air; the safe operating area is a single-pulse rating at a 150 °C junction limit, tops out near 120 A and is bounded by RDS(ON) at low drain voltage. Figures 1 to 6 are taken with pulses no longer than 300 µs at 0.5 % duty or less."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The intrinsic diode carries up to -3 A continuously and drops -0.7 V typical, -1 V max, at -1 A. Switched off from -10 A at 100 A/µs it recovers in 38 ns typical (49 ns max) with 47 nC of charge."
  }
 ],
